# One line-ending shape in the working tree, on every platform (CLOUD-612).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, measured rather than assumed. Several test gates read
# their own source with `include_str!` or `fs::read_to_string` and slice one
# item's body out of it, bounded by a column-zero `}` — the string "\n}\n".
# `include_str!` embeds the bytes of the WORKING TREE, and without this file a
# Windows checkout takes git's `core.autocrlf=true`: every line ends `\r\n`, the
# terminator matches nothing, and the slice silently widens to the rest of the
# file. On the first Windows run this repository ever did, two gates then
# asserted against ~20k characters of unrelated code and reported violations in
# functions that do not contain them.
#
# THIS IS THE BELT, NOT THE PROPERTY. Each of those sites was also fixed to
# normalize at the read and to treat an absent terminator as an error instead of
# an answer, because a gate that only holds because of a checkout setting is one
# edit to this file away from not holding. What this buys on top is the sites
# nobody has audited: the integration suites had never run on Windows at all
# (cargo stops at the first failing target), and any future `include_str!` gate
# inherits the guarantee rather than having to remember it.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect and leave binary files alone; `eol=lf` is what
# makes the checkout LF everywhere rather than only in the index. The repository
# is already LF throughout, so this normalizes no committed bytes today.
* text=auto eol=lf
